# Mathematical and package audit summary — V2 after R1

Strongest verified mathematical result: the written all-size reduction proves NP-completeness for the exact all-root, whole-orientation objective on connected simple graphs with a fully explicit cost table in `{0,1,2}`. The threshold is `K=2m+n`. Adding one to every root-arc entry adds exactly `N(N-1)` to every tree objective and its threshold, giving positive costs `{1,2,3}`.

The proof has two steps. For every tree, `q>=m` and `p+q+h=n+m+1`, so the symmetric hub-root contribution is `K+(1-h)+(q-m)`. Nonnegative remaining costs force the hub edge and exactly one clause edge at threshold. Only after that exclusion does the selected-literal identity apply. Other variable edges point hub-to-variable at a clause root, so precisely the selected variable-to-hub arc tests that clause. Both satisfiability directions are proved. No unrestricted global-optimum formula is asserted.

The mathematical gate cleared the exact audited `B=n+1` proof, with SHA-256 `2818eab189445649ae1ba98d55e3da3e5fab918de88f1779de86962ba99a3393`. The same identity at every integer `B>=2` supports the current `B=2` text; this is not a new route. The note now also states nonnegativity explicitly when describing the classical lift. Normalization includes trivial endpoints, tautology and duplicate removal, deletion of unused declarations and dense relabeling of actually occurring symbols.

Current V2 actual package runs, retained in `verification_results/actual_run_receipt.json`, passed both normal and optimized Python. The adapted checker reported **2,727,845 explicit checks per valid run**, with identical counts and mathematical outputs. The 397 formula/parameter cases and 74,997 tree/formula/parameter cases are unchanged; the added checks cover complete raw-clause validation and intended malformed-input guards. Twelve actual negative controls were rejected with nonzero exit and the intended guard: false guard, corrupt hub cost, incorrect proof hash, zero-literal suffix, boolean-literal suffix and excess-size suffix, each in normal Python and `-O`. Four temporary-copy historical replays passed: author and independent checker, each in normal and optimized Python. These preserve the inherited historical counts of 78,056 and 600,122 and their exact historical proof binding. V1's 2,727,773 adapted count is superseded history, not the current result.

The adapted suite evaluates every supplied root, including zero-cost roots, across every selected arc. A BFS evaluator and a separate fundamental-cut evaluator agree on complete per-root totals. It checks all unstructured trees, the structural count identity, individual full clause-root totals, structured total costs, both threshold directions, satisfying witnesses, cost alphabets, transpose and positive shift. Sparse numeric labels, tautologies, repeated literals, empty clauses/formulas, malformed inputs, single-vertex and two-vertex directions and the 16-tree `K4` census are checked. Every raw clause is now validated before an empty-clause shortcut. The R1 cases `[[],[0]]`, `[[],[True]]` and `[[],[1,2,3,4]]`, their reversed orders and standalone forms are rejected through both `normalize` and `construct` with the intended literal/size guard. Single-use outer and clause iterables are materialized once and checked, including a malformed suffix. The explicit four-clause example enumerates all 384 trees and finds minimum 11 against threshold 10; all 208 input costs are included in its JSON fixture, refreshed to bind the current verifier.

Finite diagnostics are corroboration and reproducibility controls. The all-size proof supplies the complexity conclusion; no enumeration claim supplies historical priority. The package's own run receipts have actual process IDs, UTC timestamps, exit codes and output byte/hash records. Hashes authenticate bytes rather than mathematical correctness.

